text:
I read `camera-api.d.ts` and `camera-notes.md` first. The notes pin down the geospatial frame: a fresh camera sits at centre `(R,0,0)` (lat 0, lon 0, note 44), north is `+Z`, and east at that centre is `-Y` (note 54). That fixes the lat/lon → world mapping to `x = R·cosφ·cosλ`, `y = −R·cosφ·sinλ`, `z = R·sinφ`, which is also exactly what makes a yaw‑90° (east) camera at the origin look toward positive longitudes. To move the centre without disturbing zoom/heading/tilt I use `setGeospatialOrientation` with only `center` (notes 7 and 11: re‑orbits at once, and keeps the fields you don't pass).

FILE: app/src/center-on.ts
```ts
import type { GeospatialCamera } from "@babylonjs/lite";
import { setGeospatialOrientation } from "@babylonjs/lite";

export function centerOnLatLon(camera: GeospatialCamera, latitudeDeg: number, longitudeDeg: number): void {
  const R = camera.limits.planetRadius;
  const lat = (latitudeDeg * Math.PI) / 180;
  const lon = (longitudeDeg * Math.PI) / 180;
  const cosLat = Math.cos(lat);
  const center = {
    x: R * cosLat * Math.cos(lon),
    y: -R * cosLat * Math.sin(lon),
    z: R * Math.sin(lat),
  };
  setGeospatialOrientation(camera, { center });
}
```

I wrote `src/center-on.ts`, which converts the degrees to radians, places the centre on the sphere of radius `camera.limits.planetRadius` using the note‑derived mapping (origin at `+X`, north `+Z`, east `−Y`), and moves it via `setGeospatialOrientation` so zoom, heading and tilt are untouched. The only things I had to infer rather than verify are that `setGeospatialOrientation` is exported from the `@babylonjs/lite` root and that passing only `center` leaves yaw/pitch/radius as they were — both are consistent with the type declaration and notes 7/11, so I'm confident, but I couldn't run anything to confirm.
